==================================================
FUNCTIONAL DEPENDENCY ACCURACY
==================================================

When explaining database functional dependencies, preserve the direction correctly.

If:

StudentID -> StudentName

this means:

Knowing StudentID is enough to determine StudentName.

It does NOT mean that knowing StudentName necessarily determines StudentID.

Explain dependencies in the correct direction.

For example:

(StudentID, CourseID) -> Grade

means the combination of StudentID and CourseID determines Grade.

StudentID -> StudentName

means StudentName depends only on StudentID.

CourseID -> CourseTitle

means CourseTitle depends only on CourseID.

Therefore StudentName and CourseTitle are partial dependencies with respect to the composite key (StudentID, CourseID).

Never reverse the dependency while explaining it in simple English.

==================================================
NORMALIZATION PRECISION
==================================================

Do not say:

"A single-column key automatically means the table is in 3NF."

That is incorrect.

Correct rule:

If all candidate keys are single-column, partial dependency cannot occur, so a relation that is already in 1NF will satisfy 2NF with respect to partial dependency.

However, it may still violate 3NF because transitive dependencies can still exist.

Example:

STUDENT(
    StudentID,
    DepartmentID,
    DepartmentName
)

StudentID -> DepartmentID
DepartmentID -> DepartmentName

The primary key is only StudentID.

There is no partial dependency, so 2NF is not the problem.

But there is a transitive dependency:

StudentID -> DepartmentID -> DepartmentName

Therefore the relation can violate 3NF even with a single-column primary key.

==================================================
DBMS NORMALIZATION ACCURACY
==================================================

When discussing normalization, preserve the formal meaning even when using simple English.

1NF:
- values should be atomic according to the intended relational design
- avoid repeating groups

2NF:
- the relation must already satisfy 1NF
- every non-prime attribute must be fully functionally dependent on every candidate key
- a partial dependency occurs when a non-prime attribute depends on only part of a composite candidate key
- if there is no composite candidate key, a partial dependency cannot occur

Do not incorrectly say that normalization always produces tables with single-column primary keys.
Composite keys are valid and may remain after decomposition.

3NF:
- the relation must satisfy 2NF
- avoid transitive dependencies of non-prime attributes on keys through other non-prime attributes
- explain the functional dependency chain explicitly when useful

For example:

(StudentID, CourseID) -> Grade
StudentID -> StudentName
CourseID -> CourseName

StudentName and CourseName are partial dependencies because they depend on only one part of the composite key.

Correct decomposition:

STUDENT(StudentID, StudentName)

COURSE(CourseID, CourseName)

ENROLLMENT(StudentID, CourseID, Grade)

Use examples like this when explaining 2NF.

